  await app.init();

  const gqlSchemaFactory = app.get(GraphQLSchemaFactory);
  const schema = await gqlSchemaFactory.create([RecipesResolver]);
  console.log(printSchema(schema));
}
```

#### Usage#
The gqlSchemaFactory.create() method takes an array of resolver class references. For example:

CODE EXAMPLE
LANGUAGE: typescript

```
const schema = await gqlSchemaFactory.create([
  RecipesResolver,
  AuthorsResolver,
  PostsResolver,
]);
```

It also takes an optional second argument with an array of scalar classes:

CODE EXAMPLE
LANGUAGE: typescript

```
const schema = await gqlSchemaFactory.create(
  [RecipesResolver, AuthorsResolver, PostsResolver],
  [DurationScalar, DateScalar],
);
```

Lastly, you can pass an options object (the same BuildSchemaOptions accepted by the buildSchemaOptions property of the GraphQLModule configuration):

CODE EXAMPLE
LANGUAGE: typescript

```
const schema = await gqlSchemaFactory.create([RecipesResolver], {
  skipCheck: true,
  orphanedTypes: [],
});
```

- skipCheck: skip schema validation; boolean, defaults to false
- orphanedTypes: an array of classes to generate even though they aren't explicitly referenced (i.e., aren't part of the object graph). Normally, a class that is declared but not referenced anywhere in the graph is omitted.

### Interfaces
Like many type systems, GraphQL supports interfaces. An interface is an abstract type that defines a set of fields that a type must include to implement the interface (see interfaces in the GraphQL documentation).

#### Code first#
In the code first approach, you define a GraphQL interface by creating an abstract class annotated with the @InterfaceType() decorator, exported from the @nestjs/graphql package.

CODE EXAMPLE
LANGUAGE: typescript

```
import { Field, ID, InterfaceType } from '@nestjs/graphql';

@InterfaceType()
export abstract class Character {
  @Field(() => ID)
  id: string;

  @Field()
  name: string;
}
```

This generates the following part of the GraphQL schema in SDL:

CODE EXAMPLE
LANGUAGE: graphql

```
interface Character {
  id: ID!
  name: String!
}
```

To implement the Character interface, use the implements key:

CODE EXAMPLE
LANGUAGE: typescript

```
@ObjectType({
  implements: () => [Character],
})
export class Human implements Character {
  id: string;
  name: string;
}
```

The default resolveType() function generated by the library determines the type based on the value returned from the resolver method. This means that you must return class instances, not plain JavaScript object literals.
To provide a custom resolveType() function, pass the resolveType property in the options object of the @InterfaceType() decorator:

CODE EXAMPLE
LANGUAGE: typescript

```
@InterfaceType({
  resolveType(book) {
    if (book.colors) {
      return ColoringBook;
    }
    return TextBook;
  },
})
export abstract class Book {
  @Field(() => ID)
  id: string;

  @Field()
  title: string;
}
```

